.TH TERM-PANE-STATE 1
.SH NAME
term-pane-state \- publish per-pane effective tmux work and attention state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B term-pane-state --socket SOCKET --session ID --print
.br
.B term-pane-state --socket SOCKET --session ID
.br
.B term-pane-state --socket SOCKET --client NAME --client-pid PID --client-created EPOCH
.SH DESCRIPTION
Summarizes the panes in exactly one session. The embedding tmux configuration
defines the structured predicates @pane_busy, @pane_pending, @pane_waiting and
@pane_failed; the same predicates should drive its status display. Waiting and
failed must imply pending. A configuration that predates @pane_waiting expands
it empty, which counts as no waiting panes.
.PP
Counts are per-pane effective states chosen by the publisher. Each pane counts
once, as the first of: failed, waiting, done (pending without either), working
(busy without attention). A pane with attention does not also count as
working, but it never hides another pane. A pane that is both failed and
waiting counts as failed.
.PP
The print form emits a versioned JSON object with integer v, working, pending,
failed and waiting fields. pending counts every pane with attention; failed
and waiting are disjoint parts of it, so failed + waiting <= pending and the
remainder is done. A failed query or invalid snapshot emits nothing and exits 1.
.PP
The client form resolves an exact live non-control client on the named socket,
summarizes its session and delegates publication of the TERM_PANE_STATE WezTerm
user variable to Termnav. Its payload also carries
a changing heartbeat integer. The UI should expire state based on local time
since receipt, not compare clocks across hosts. No status text is printed.
.PP
The session form, --session without --print, publishes the same way to every
attached non-control client of the session, each evaluated with its own
selected pane and focus. A session with no such client exits 0 with nothing
to do. A malformed client row or a client that fails to publish makes it exit
1 after trying the others.
.PP
For the client's selected pane, the embedding config's pending, waiting and
failed predicates are evaluated with that client's focus context. The print form
remains a session-wide snapshot. Other panes and fresh child summaries keep
their attention even when the selected pane's local mark is hidden.
.PP
Child layers publish through Termnav pane leases on the parent's wall clock:
@termnav_child_attention holds expiry:working:pending:failed and
@termnav_child_waiting holds expiry:N, the child's waiting panes. A waiting
lease counts only beside a valid attention lease whose pending minus failed
covers N. A malformed, expired or unbounded lease contributes nothing; a
wire-shaped one is removed with a compare-and-clear on its exact pane, so a
renewed lease survives, and other text is left untouched.
.PP
Use this short-lived helper in a client-specific tmux status #() job. tmux
serializes identical jobs; status-interval supplies bounded reconciliation
without a resident watcher or a process per pane. A query is not acknowledgment:
the existing pane focus policy remains authoritative.
.PP
tmux reruns a status job at most once a second, and otherwise only on the
status interval, so a focus or selection change could reach the terminal
seconds late. Run the session form from the tmux hooks for those events with
.B run-shell -b
and discard its output, since a background run-shell prints output and
failures into a pane.
.BR term-pane-mark (1)
does the same after each mark change once the configuration sets
.B @term_pane_state_publish
to
.BR on .
.PP
Publishing requires Termnav's tmux attention route to verify foreground
ownership and compose state through immediate local or SSH parents, or publish
directly with its bounded native terminal writer. The shell never writes to a
client tty. Legacy Termnav status 3 fails closed and requires an update; a
missing or failed transport emits nothing. The helper passes --waiting to
Termnav when any pane is waiting; if Termnav exits 2 because it predates that
flag, the helper retries once without it, and that layer shows waiting
attention as done. The print-only summary remains
available standalone.
.PP
Snapshots are advisory display state. A delayed snapshot can briefly replace
a newer visual state; the next successful poll repairs it. Child leases and
the GUI must expire state when updates stop. No lifecycle operation is driven
by these badges.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 indicates success; 1 indicates missing, invalid or changed runtime state;
64 indicates invalid arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR term-pane-mark (1),
.BR tmux (1)
